```java
@Override
public void onBackPressed() {
    DrawerLayout drawer = findViewById(R.id.drawer_layout);
    if (drawer.isDrawerOpen(GravityCompat.START)) {
        drawer.closeDrawer(GravityCompat.START);
    } else {
        Fragment currentFragment = getSupportFragmentManager()
                .findFragmentById(R.id.content);
        if (currentFragment instanceof HomeFragment) {
            checkDoubleBackPress();
        } else {
            Fragment fragment = new HomeFragment();
            getSupportFragmentManager().beginTransaction().replace(R.id.content, fragment).commit();
            setDefaultMenuSelected(0);
        }
    }
}
```

The issue is that when `ACTION_GET_CONTENT` is triggered via `startActivityForResult()`, the system file picker launches as a new activity on top of the fragment, but the back handler doesn't check for this overlay—it just replaces the fragment with HomeFragment instead of letting the file picker dismiss naturally.

I need to track when the file picker is active and override the back behavior accordingly. The challenge is that fragments like `InvertPdfFragment` and `RemovePagesFragment` use `startActivityForResult()` to launch the picker, but I don't have direct access to them, so I need a different approach to detect when the picker dialog is open.

Looking at the exploration results, the MorphingButton is what triggers the file picker when clicked, and the button gets set to a selected state once a file is chosen. So I could either track the button state directly or monitor the lifecycle of the file picker activity itself to know when to allow normal back behavior versus navigating to the home fragment. I see the issue now—the file picker launches as a separate activity on top of MainActivity, so when the user presses back, it dismisses the picker naturally. But if MainActivity becomes the foreground activity again (perhaps due to a lifecycle event), the default back handler would pop it from the stack and show HomeFragment instead of staying where it was. The user's expectation is that the slider should just collapse on back press, keeping them on the same fragment, but the actual behavior navigates them away. I need to check how the fragments are implementing the MorphingButton click handler to see if they're using `startActivityForResult()` or `registerForActivityResult()` for the file picker, and then I can implement a flag in the base fragment to track whether the picker is currently active so the back press can dismiss it instead of navigating away.

